            <ArticleTitle>Hospitalization for congestive heart failure: United States, 2000-2010.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Congestive heart failure (CHF) is a serious medical condition in which the heart cannot pump enough blood to meet the body's needs. CHF is often caused by hypertension, diabetes, or coronary heart disease. It is estimated that 5.8 million people in the United States have CHF (1). CHF is one of the most common reasons those aged 65 and over are hospitalized (2). This report presents National Hospital Discharge Survey (NHDS) data from 2000 through 2010 on hospitalizations for CHF.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>All material appearing in this report is in the public domain and may be reproduced or copied without permission; citation as to source, however, is appreciated.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
